  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 111 AM MDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nLow pressure off the coast of central California will remain in\nplace today. It will start moving inland on Thursday, passing \noverhead Friday, before exiting the region on Saturday. Models \nshow a batch of showers lifting northward through our area this \nmorning but then clearing out for the afternoon with a few \nisolated showers developing late. We will probably see another \nwave of light showers Wednesday night into Thursday morning. But\nthe main batch of precipitation will hold off until Thursday \nafternoon and evening. Models show a healthy band of showers and\nthunderstorms on the east side of the low as it moves \nnorthward. This is what will likely produce most of the \nprecipitation over our area. There is also a good area of \nstratiform deformation zone rain on the north and northwest side\nof the low that will mainly pass through the central mountains \ninto the Island Park region. Models are showing a good bit of \ndry air in the interior part of the low which could influence \nrainfall amounts. Forecast rainfall amounts remain relatively \nunchanged. The mean and 50th percentile qpf from the NBM shows \n1.00 to 1.50 inches for east Idaho and across the Arco Desert \ninto the Wood River and Lost River areas. The Magic Valley and \ncentral mountains look drier with around 0.50 inches. The 10th \npercentile is as low as 0.10 inches for the Magic Valley with \n0.25 to 0.60 inches elsewhere. The 90th percentile is especially\nwet with amounts ranging from 1.25 inches to 2.00 inches. Both \nthe operational GFS and NAM show this drier air in the \nreflectivity fields so the 90th percentile looks highly \nunlikely. But a drier than advertised system looks more \nplausible based on the dry air we are seeing in the models. The \nWPC forecast is just a tad under the 50% percentile. Snowfall \nwith this system continues to look mostly unimpactful. Perhaps \nsnow levels fall to some of our higher passes Friday night as \nthe colder air settles into the area before the precipitation \nexits.\n\nCooler and drier air arrives for the weekend. We will notice about a \n20 degree drop in temperatures from our highs on Thursday to highs \nfor Friday. Temps will rebound slightly for next week, but remaining \nnear or slightly below normal.",
